---

<section class="om-reference adjust" aria-label="Adjusting the pie chart result">
  <fieldset class="om-tabs">
    <legend>Choose an adjustment</legend>

    <input class="om-radio" type="radio" name="adjust-tab" id="adjust-demand" checked />
    <label class="om-tab" for="adjust-demand">
      <span class="om-tab-number">1</span><span>Demand</span>
    </label>

    <input class="om-radio" type="radio" name="adjust-tab" id="adjust-strength" />
    <label class="om-tab" for="adjust-strength">
      <span class="om-tab-number">2</span><span>Strength</span>
    </label>

    <input class="om-radio" type="radio" name="adjust-tab" id="adjust-stacked" />
    <label class="om-tab" for="adjust-stacked">
      <span class="om-tab-number">3</span><span>Two multipliers</span>
    </label>

    <div class="om-panel" data-tab="demand">
      <div class="om-block">
        <h2>Demand = dose &minus; residual</h2>
        <p>
          The chlorine that got <strong>used up</strong> &mdash; eaten by iron,
          ammonia and organics &mdash; before anything was left over to measure.
          Adjust this <strong>before</strong> the chart, on the way in.
        </p>

        <div class="om-scenario">
          <span class="om-scenario-label">END OF SHIFT</span>
          <p>
            Your feed is set for <strong>3.0 mg/L</strong> into <strong>1.0 MGD</strong>. Post-contact residual reads <strong>0.5 mg/L</strong>. The water ate the difference.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="om-equation" aria-label="3.0 minus 0.5 equals 2.5 milligrams per liter of demand">
          <div class="om-card"><strong>3.0</strong><span>DOSE<br />FED</span></div>
          <span class="om-op" aria-hidden="true">&minus;</span>
          <div class="om-card is-subtracted"><strong>0.5</strong><span>RESIDUAL<br />LEFT</span></div>
          <span class="om-op is-equals" aria-hidden="true">=</span>
          <div class="om-card is-result"><strong>2.5</strong><span>MG/L<br />DEMAND</span></div>
        </div>

        <p>Now run that through the chart:</p>

        <div class="om-equation" aria-label="2.5 times 8.34 times 1.0 equals 21 pounds per day">
          <div class="om-card"><strong>2.5</strong><span>DEMAND<br />MG/L</span></div>
          <span class="om-op" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
          <div class="om-card is-conversion"><strong>8.34</strong><span>LB PER<br />GALLON</span></div>
          <span class="om-op" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
          <div class="om-card"><strong>1.0</strong><span>FLOW<br />MGD</span></div>
          <span class="om-op is-equals" aria-hidden="true">=</span>
          <div class="om-card is-result"><strong>21</strong><span>LB/DAY CONSUMED</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="om-note is-watch">
          <span class="om-note-label">WATCH THIS NUMBER</span>
          <p>
            Demand is a <strong>water quality signal</strong>, not just
            bookkeeping. Track it shift to shift. If demand climbs while your
            flow holds steady, something upstream changed &mdash; a slug of
            ammonia, iron breaking through, a line that got stirred up.
          </p>
          <p>
            Feeding <strong>3.0</strong> and consuming <strong>2.5</strong> is
            different from feeding 3.0 and consuming 1.0. Same feed rate on the
            invoice; very different plant.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="om-panel" data-tab="strength">
      <div class="om-block">
        <h2>Your chemical is not pure</h2>
        <p>
          The chart tells you the pounds of <strong>chlorine</strong> you need.
          It does not tell you how much <strong>product</strong> to pump. Adjust
          this <strong>after</strong> the chart, on the way out.
        </p>

        <div class="om-scenario">
          <span class="om-scenario-label">SIZING THE DAY TANK</span>
          <p>
            You need <strong>25 lb/day</strong> of chlorine. What is in the tote
            is <strong>12.5% sodium hypochlorite</strong>. How much liquid
            actually has to move?
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          Hypochlorite is only 12.5&#37; chlorine &mdash; the rest is water and
          caustic. Divide by the decimal to get pounds of product:
        </p>

        <p class="om-inline-math">25 &divide; 0.125 = <b>200 lb/day of solution</b></p>

        <div class="om-note is-check">
          <span class="om-note-label">DOES IT MAKE SENSE?</span>
          <p>
            Weaker product means you feed <strong>more</strong> of it. Eight
            times the weight here. If your answer came out <strong>smaller</strong> than the pounds of chlorine, you multiplied
            when you should have divided.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="om-note is-watch">
          <span class="om-note-label">ON THE FLOOR</span>
          <p>
            Size pumps, day tanks and delivery schedules against the <strong>200</strong>, never the 25. This is also why hypo plants run
            out faster than the chlorine numbers suggest &mdash; and why
            hypo that has been sitting all summer no longer feeds at label
            strength.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="om-panel" data-tab="stacked">
      <div class="om-block">
        <h2>Two multipliers stacked</h2>
        <p>
          Same rule as strength &mdash; it just runs twice. Some products are
          rated on <strong>purity</strong> and again on how much of the useful
          ion they carry.
        </p>

        <div class="om-scenario">
          <span class="om-scenario-label">FLUORIDE FEED</span>
          <p>
            You are holding <strong>0.8 mg/L</strong> fluoride in <strong>3.0 MGD</strong> using sodium fluorosilicate &mdash; <strong>98.5% pure</strong>, <strong>60.6% available fluoride</strong>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="om-steps">STEP 1 &mdash; POUNDS OF FLUORIDE YOU ACTUALLY NEED</p>

        <div class="om-equation" aria-label="0.8 times 8.34 times 3.0 equals 20 pounds per day of fluoride">
          <div class="om-card"><strong>0.8</strong><span>DOSE<br />MG/L</span></div>
          <span class="om-op" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
          <div class="om-card is-conversion"><strong>8.34</strong><span>LB PER<br />GALLON</span></div>
          <span class="om-op" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
          <div class="om-card"><strong>3.0</strong><span>FLOW<br />MGD</span></div>
          <span class="om-op is-equals" aria-hidden="true">=</span>
          <div class="om-card is-result"><strong>20.0</strong><span>LB/DAY FLUORIDE</span></div>
        </div>

        <p class="om-steps">STEP 2 &mdash; DIVIDE BY WHAT FRACTION IS REALLY FLUORIDE</p>

        <div class="om-equation" aria-label="20 divided by the quantity 0.985 times 0.606 equals 33.5 pounds per day of product">
          <div class="om-card"><strong>20.0</strong><span>LB/DAY<br />NEEDED</span></div>
          <span class="om-op is-divide" aria-hidden="true">&divide;</span>
          <div class="om-group">
            <div class="om-card"><strong>0.985</strong><span>PURITY</span></div>
            <span class="om-op" aria-hidden="true">&times;</span>
            <div class="om-card"><strong>0.606</strong><span>AVAILABLE<br />FLUORIDE</span></div>
          </div>
          <span class="om-op is-equals" aria-hidden="true">=</span>
          <div class="om-card is-result"><strong>33.5</strong><span>LB/DAY PRODUCT</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="om-note is-check">
          <span class="om-note-label">THE RULE NEVER CHANGES</span>
          <p>
            One divisor or two, you always feed <strong>more product</strong> than pure chemical. The answer must come out larger than the pounds
            you needed. Here: 20.0 needed, 33.5 fed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </fieldset>
</section>

<style>
  /* Tab switching stays CSS-only so the page works before hydration. */
  .adjust #adjust-demand:checked ~ .om-panel[data-tab="demand"],
  .adjust #adjust-strength:checked ~ .om-panel[data-tab="strength"],
  .adjust #adjust-stacked:checked ~ .om-panel[data-tab="stacked"] {
    display: block;
    animation: om-panel-enter 260ms cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1) both;
  }

  .adjust .om-panel {
    width: 100%;
  }
</style>
